#Question 05:
The length of the set is 4.

Python sets cannot contain duplicate values. When you define values = {1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4}, Python automatically removes the repeating numbers. In memory, the set becomes {1, 2, 3, 4}, which contains exactly 4 elements.

#Question 06:

A = {1, 2, 3, 4}
B = {3, 4, 5, 6}

print("Unique elements in A and B:", A | B)
print("Elements common to both sets:", A & B)

print("A - B:", A - B)
print("B - A:", B - A)

print("Elements in either A or B, but not both:", A ^ B)

• A - B starts with set A and takes away any numbers that also appear in B. This leaves only the items unique to A ({1, 2}).
• B - A starts with set B and takes away any numbers that also appear in A. This leaves only the items unique to B ({5, 6}).
• Since set A and set B do not share the exact same starting elements, changing the order of subtraction gives different answers.
